#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:1
msgid "Railroad-diagram Generator"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:4
msgid ""
"This is a small js library for generating railroad diagrams (like what "
"[JSON.org](http://json.org) uses) using SVG."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:8
msgid ""
"Railroad diagrams are a way of visually representing a grammar in a form "
"that is more readable than using regular expressions or BNF. I think "
"(though I haven't given it a lot of thought yet) that if it's easy to "
"write a context-free grammar for the language, the corresponding railroad"
" diagram will be easy as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:13
msgid ""
"There are several railroad-diagram generators out there, but none of them"
" had the visual appeal I wanted. [Here's an example of how they "
"look!](http://www.xanthir.com/etc/railroad-diagrams/example.html) And "
"[here's an online generator for you to play with and get SVG code "
"from!](http://www.xanthir.com/etc/railroad-diagrams/generator.html)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:17
msgid ""
"The library now exists in a Python port as well!  See the information "
"further down."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:19
msgid "Details"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:22
msgid ""
"To use the library, just include the js and css files, and then call the "
"Diagram() function. Its arguments are the components of the diagram "
"(Diagram is a special form of Sequence). An alternative to Diagram() is "
"ComplexDiagram() which is used to describe a complex type diagram. "
"Components are either leaves or containers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:27
msgid "The leaves:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:28
msgid "Terminal(text) or a bare string - represents literal text"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:29
msgid "NonTerminal(text) - represents an instruction or another production"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:30
msgid "Comment(text) - a comment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:31
msgid "Skip() - an empty line"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:33
msgid "The containers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:34
msgid "Sequence(children) - like simple concatenation in a regex"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:35
msgid ""
"Choice(index, children) - like | in a regex.  The index argument "
"specifies which child is the \"normal\" choice and should go in the "
"middle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:36
msgid ""
"Optional(child, skip) - like ? in a regex.  A shorthand for `Choice(1, "
"[Skip(), child])`.  If the optional `skip` parameter has the value "
"`\"skip\"`, it instead puts the Skip() in the straight-line path, for "
"when the \"normal\" behavior is to omit the item."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:37
msgid ""
"OneOrMore(child, repeat) - like + in a regex.  The 'repeat' argument is "
"optional, and specifies something that must go between the repetitions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:38
msgid ""
"ZeroOrMore(child, repeat, skip) - like * in a regex.  A shorthand for "
"`Optional(OneOrMore(child, repeat))`.  The optional `skip` parameter is "
"identical to Optional()."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:40
msgid ""
"For convenience, each component can be called with or without `new`. If "
"called without `new`, the container components become n-ary; that is, you"
" can say either `new Sequence([A, B])` or just `Sequence(A,B)`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:45
msgid ""
"After constructing a Diagram, call `.format(...padding)` on it, "
"specifying 0-4 padding values (just like CSS) for some additional "
"\"breathing space\" around the diagram (the paddings default to 20px)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:47
msgid ""
"The result can either be `.toString()`'d for the markup, or `.toSVG()`'d "
"for an `<svg>` element, which can then be immediately inserted to the "
"document.  As a convenience, Diagram also has an `.addTo(element)` "
"method, which immediately converts it to SVG and appends it to the "
"referenced element with default paddings. `element` defaults to "
"`document.body`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:49
msgid "Options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:52
msgid ""
"There are a few options you can tweak, at the bottom of the file.  Just "
"tweak either until the diagram looks like what you want. You can also "
"change the CSS file - feel free to tweak to your heart's content. Note, "
"though, that if you change the text sizes in the CSS, you'll have to go "
"adjust the metrics for the leaf nodes as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:57
msgid ""
"VERTICAL_SEPARATION - sets the minimum amount of vertical separation "
"between two items.  Note that the stroke width isn't counted when "
"computing the separation; this shouldn't be relevant unless you have a "
"very small separation or very large stroke width."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:58
msgid ""
"ARC_RADIUS - the radius of the arcs used in the branching containers like"
" Choice.  This has a relatively large effect on the size of non-trivial "
"diagrams.  Both tight and loose values look good, depending on what "
"you're going for."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:59
msgid ""
"DIAGRAM_CLASS - the class set on the root `<svg>` element of each "
"diagram, for use in the CSS stylesheet."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:60
msgid ""
"STROKE_ODD_PIXEL_LENGTH - the default stylesheet uses odd pixel lengths "
"for 'stroke'. Due to rasterization artifacts, they look best when the "
"item has been translated half a pixel in both directions. If you change "
"the styling to use a stroke with even pixel lengths, you'll want to set "
"this variable to `false`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:61
msgid ""
"INTERNAL_ALIGNMENT - when some branches of a container are narrower than "
"others, this determines how they're aligned in the extra space.  Defaults"
" to \"center\", but can be set to \"left\" or \"right\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:63
msgid "Caveats"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:66
msgid ""
"At this early stage, the generator is feature-complete and works as "
"intended, but still has several TODOs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:68
msgid ""
"The font-sizes are hard-coded right now, and the font handling in general"
" is very dumb - I'm just guessing at some metrics that are probably "
"\"good enough\" rather than measuring things properly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:70
msgid "Python Port"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:73
msgid ""
"In addition to the canonical JS version, the library now exists as a "
"Python library as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:75
msgid ""
"Using it is basically identical.  The config variables are globals in the"
" file, and so may be adjusted either manually or via tweaking from inside"
" your program."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/railroad-diagrams_README.md:77
msgid ""
"The main difference from the JS port is how you extract the string from "
"the Diagram.  You'll find a `writeSvg(writerFunc)` method on `Diagram`, "
"which takes a callback of one argument and passes it the string form of "
"the diagram.  For example, it can be used like "
"`Diagram(...).writeSvg(sys.stdout.write)` to write to stdout.  **Note**: "
"the callback will be called multiple times as it builds up the string, "
"not just once with the whole thing.  If you need it all at once, consider"
" something like a `StringIO` as an easy way to collect it into a single "
"string."
msgstr ""

